From 5c3a60a74cd2d63d0391bbc62e278aedd8515f57 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: DogukanDG <dagtekindogukan1@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 9 Sep 2026 13:05:07 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 7/8] Stop computing resource utilization nothing reads

Some samples never finished inside a 900 s timeout, and the cost was not in
the simulation. On the sample that kept failing (3000 cases, 611 resource
instances, a 164-day horizon) the simulation reached its end at 80 s and the
run was still going 800 s later, entirely inside StatisticsLogger:

    Scylla.main
      SimulationManager.run:186
        StatisticsLogger.writeToLog:103
          DateTimeUtils.getAvailabilityTime

getAvailabilityTime walks a resource's timetable window by window from the
start of the simulation to its end, and writeToLog calls it once per resource
instance. Its cost is therefore instances times horizon, independent of how
much simulating there is to do -- which is why it appeared only after two
earlier fixes, honouring the `amount` field (47 resources became 611
instances) and emitting an end date (a 164-day horizon), were both correct and
both necessary.

Only the <resources> section of the output reads the result. The process
metrics we consume -- flow_time, effective, waiting -- are computed separately
and are unaffected: measured across a 2400-case A/B run, all twelve reported
figures are bit-identical with the calculation on and off.

Off by default it is not; callers ask for it with
-Dscylla.resourceAvailability=off, matching how the XES logger is switched.
The failing 3000-case sample now finishes in 54 s.

Also drops the call counters added to the eligibility plugin while tracking
this down. They did their job: the counter stopped rising at 80 s, which is
what showed the simulation had finished and the time was going somewhere else.

